      "Abstract": {
        "CopyrightInformation": "© 2012 John Wiley & Sons Ltd.",
        "AbstractText": [
          {
            "NlmCategory": "OBJECTIVE",
            "Label": "OBJECTIVE",
            "content": "Elevated YKL-40 concentrations have been observed in both coronary heart disease (CHD) and diabetes. Thus, YKL-40 may play a role in pathogenesis of CHD in patients with diabetes. We evaluated whether plasma YKL-40 concentration can predict all-cause and cardiovascular mortality in individuals with type 2 diabetes."
          },
          {
            "NlmCategory": "METHODS",
            "Label": "DESIGN",
            "content": "This is a prospective, observational study."
          },
          {
            "NlmCategory": "METHODS",
            "Label": "PATIENTS",
            "content": "A total of 628 subjects with type 2 diabetes were recruited between July 1996 and June 2003."
          },
          {
            "NlmCategory": "METHODS",
            "Label": "MEASUREMENTS",
            "content": "Plasma YKL-40 concentrations were measured via enzyme-linked immunosorbent assay (ELISA). The cohort was followed up until 31 December 2008, when vital status and causes of death were obtained. Survival analysis and concordance statistics were performed. All-cause and cardiovascular mortalities were documented."
          },
          {
            "NlmCategory": "RESULTS",
            "Label": "RESULTS",
            "content": "There were 153 (24·36%) mortalities, including 48 participants (7·64%) who died from cardiovascular diseases (CVDs). Participants with higher plasma YKL-40 (defined with a level above the median of 87·5 μg/l) had an increased risk of mortality. After adjusting for confounding variables, the hazard ratios (HR) for all-cause and cardiovascular mortality in participants with higher plasma YKL-40 were 1·97 (95% CI, 1·31-2·95, P < 0·01) and 2·45 (95% CI, 1·11-5·37, P < 0·05). The results remained similar after adjustment for age. Concordance statistics revealed that plasma YKL-40 concentration significantly increases the predictive power for both all-cause and cardiovascular mortality in different models."
          },
          {
            "NlmCategory": "CONCLUSIONS",
            "Label": "CONCLUSIONS",
            "content": "Plasma YKL-40 concentration is an independent predictor of 10-year all-cause and cardiovascular mortality in subjects with type 2 diabetes. Further investigations on the role of YKL-40 in the pathogenesis of CVD are required to elucidate the underlying mechanisms."
          }
        ]
      },
